        "---\n",
        "title: \"Naive Bayes and Text Classification\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Bayes decision rules, multinomial likelihoods, smoothing, and linear log-odds\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Text classification is the supervised learning problem of assigning a label to a document.  A document may be an email, review, abstract, news article, student question, or clinical note.  In this chapter we develop one of the most important classical models for this task: **Naive Bayes**.  The model is simple, fast, interpretable, and mathematically transparent.  Its main lesson is that a probabilistic model for documents can lead to a linear decision rule in word-count features.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. formulate text classification as a statistical decision problem;\n",
        "2. derive the Bayes classifier under zero-one loss;\n",
        "3. define the multinomial Naive Bayes model for count vectors;\n",
        "4. estimate class priors and class-conditional token probabilities from data;\n",
        "5. explain the conditional independence assumption and why it is called \"naive\";\n",
        "6. derive the log-score form\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   s_c(x)=\\log \\widehat \\pi_c+\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j\\log \\widehat \\theta_{cj};\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "7. show that Naive Bayes is a linear classifier in count-vector space;\n",
        "8. implement Naive Bayes from scratch in Python;\n",
        "9. use add-$\\alpha$ smoothing to avoid zero probabilities;\n",
        "10. evaluate a text classifier with confusion matrices, precision, recall, and $F_1$ score;\n",
        "11. use AI tools to critique assumptions, detect data leakage, and explain classifier behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Naive Bayes matters\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes is not a modern large language model.  However, it remains a useful mathematical baseline for NLP because it makes the modeling assumptions explicit.\n",
        "\n",
        "The model answers a clean question:\n",
        "\n",
        "> If documents in class $c$ generate tokens according to a class-specific probability distribution, which class most likely generated a new document?\n",
        "\n",
        "This question leads naturally to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Bayes' theorem;\n",
        "- maximum likelihood estimation;\n",
        "- multinomial distributions;\n",
        "- Dirichlet/additive smoothing;\n",
        "- log-likelihood ratios;\n",
        "- linear decision boundaries;\n",
        "- interpretable feature weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern neural classifiers often achieve higher accuracy, but Naive Bayes gives a clear first model against which more complicated systems should be compared.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Central idea.**  A count vector $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)\\in \\mathbb{N}^d$ records how often each vocabulary token appears in a document.  Multinomial Naive Bayes estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=c\\mid X=x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "by combining a class prior $P(Y=c)$ with a class-conditional token model $P(X=x\\mid Y=c)$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Text classification as a supervised learning problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{Y}=\\{1,2,\\ldots,K\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be a finite label set.  Examples include:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Task | Input | Label set |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| sentiment analysis | movie review | $\\{\\text{negative},\\text{positive}\\}$ |\n",
        "| spam detection | email | $\\{\\text{ham},\\text{spam}\\}$ |\n",
        "| topic classification | news article | $\\{\\text{sports},\\text{business},\\text{science},\\ldots\\}$ |\n",
        "| intent classification | student question | $\\{\\text{transfer credit},\\text{course registration},\\text{visa},\\ldots\\}$ |\n",
        "\n",
        "A training dataset is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_n=\\{(d_i,y_i)\\}_{i=1}^n,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $d_i$ is a document and $y_i\\in \\mathcal{Y}$ is its label.  After choosing a representation map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi: \\text{documents}\\longrightarrow \\mathbb{R}^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "we obtain feature vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=\\phi(d_i)\\in \\mathbb{R}^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For Naive Bayes, the most common representation is a count vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=(x_{i1},x_{i2},\\ldots,x_{id})\\in \\mathbb{N}^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $x_{ij}$ is the number of times vocabulary token $v_j$ appears in document $d_i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is to learn a classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f:\\mathbb{N}^d\\longrightarrow \\mathcal{Y}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Bayes classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume $(X,Y)$ is a random pair with $X\\in \\mathbb{N}^d$ and $Y\\in \\mathcal{Y}$.  Under zero-one loss,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\widehat y,y)=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "0, & \\widehat y=y,\\\\\n",
        "1, & \\widehat y\\neq y,\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the best possible prediction rule, if the true distribution were known, is the **Bayes classifier**:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f^*(x)=\\arg\\max_{c\\in \\mathcal{Y}} P(Y=c\\mid X=x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This rule chooses the class with the largest posterior probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "By Bayes' theorem,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=c\\mid X=x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{P(X=x\\mid Y=c)P(Y=c)}{P(X=x)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For classification, the denominator $P(X=x)$ does not depend on $c$.  Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f^*(x)=\\arg\\max_{c\\in \\mathcal{Y}} P(X=x\\mid Y=c)P(Y=c).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the maximum a posteriori, or MAP, decision rule.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Bayes decision rule.**  To classify $x$, compare the posterior scores\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X=x\\mid Y=c)P(Y=c), \\qquad c=1,\\ldots,K.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes becomes a usable classifier after we specify and estimate the class-conditional distribution $P(X=x\\mid Y=c)$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## The multinomial event model\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_d\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the vocabulary.  In the multinomial Naive Bayes model, each class $c$ has a token probability vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_c=(\\theta_{c1},\\theta_{c2},\\ldots,\\theta_{cd})\\in \\Delta^{d-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{cj}\\ge 0, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The interpretation is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{cj}=P(\\text{a randomly drawn token is }v_j\\mid Y=c).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now suppose a document has length\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m=\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The multinomial model assumes that, conditional on the class, the document's tokens are generated by repeated draws from $\\theta_c$.  The probability of observing the count vector $x$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X=x\\mid Y=c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{m!}{x_1!x_2!\\cdots x_d!}\n",
        "\\prod_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}^{x_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The multinomial coefficient counts how many token sequences have the same count vector.\n",
        "\n",
        "For classification, if the document $x$ is fixed, the coefficient\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{m!}{x_1!x_2!\\cdots x_d!}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "does not depend on the class $c$.  Therefore the MAP classifier can ignore it:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat y\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\max_{c\\in\\mathcal{Y}}\n",
        "\\pi_c\\prod_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}^{x_j},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\pi_c=P(Y=c)$ is the class prior.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The conditional independence assumption\n",
        "\n",
        "The word \"naive\" refers to the conditional independence assumption.  In sequence form, if a document has tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_m,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the model assumes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\mid Y=c)\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^m P(w_t\\mid Y=c).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "That is, after conditioning on the class label, the tokens are treated as independent draws from a class-specific distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a strong assumption.  Natural language has grammar, word order, collocations, phrases, and long-range dependence.  For example, in real text,\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "not good\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "is not the same as the two independent words `not` and `good`.  Yet Naive Bayes often works surprisingly well for topic and sentiment tasks because the presence of highly class-indicative words can dominate the classification score.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "The Naive Bayes assumption is usually false as a generative model of language.  The important question is not whether the model is literally true, but whether the resulting decision rule is useful and interpretable for a particular task.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Estimating the parameters\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we have training examples\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(x_i,y_i),\\qquad i=1,\\ldots,n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Class priors\n",
        "\n",
        "The maximum likelihood estimate of the class prior is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\pi_c\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{n_c}{n},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "n_c=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y_i=c\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is the number of training documents in class $c$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Token probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "N_{cj}=\\sum_{i:y_i=c} x_{ij}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the total number of times token $v_j$ appears in documents with label $c$.  Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "N_c=\\sum_{j=1}^d N_{cj}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the total number of tokens in class $c$.  The maximum likelihood estimate is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\theta_{cj}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{N_{cj}}{N_c}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Derivation by constrained optimization\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed class $c$, the likelihood for $\\theta_c$ is proportional to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L_c(\\theta_c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}^{N_{cj}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_c(\\theta_c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d N_{cj}\\log \\theta_{cj},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "subject to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using a Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_c,\\lambda)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d N_{cj}\\log \\theta_{cj}\n",
        "+\\lambda\\left(1-\\sum_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The stationarity condition gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\theta_{cj}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{N_{cj}}{\\theta_{cj}}-\\lambda=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{cj}=\\frac{N_{cj}}{\\lambda}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Summing over $j$ gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1=\\sum_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\sum_{j=1}^d N_{cj}=\\frac{N_c}{\\lambda},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so $\\lambda=N_c$.  Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\theta_{cj}=\\frac{N_{cj}}{N_c}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Zero probabilities and add-$\\alpha$ smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "The maximum likelihood estimator assigns probability zero to any token that never appears in class $c$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "N_{cj}=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\widehat \\theta_{cj}=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This creates a serious problem.  If a test document contains even one such token, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\prod_{j=1}^d \\widehat \\theta_{cj}^{x_j}=0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for that class.  The model may rule out a class entirely because of one unseen word.\n",
        "\n",
        "To avoid this, we use add-$\\alpha$ smoothing:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\theta_{cj}^{(\\alpha)}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{N_{cj}+\\alpha}{N_c+\\alpha d},\n",
        "\\qquad \\alpha>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Common choices include $\\alpha=1$ for Laplace smoothing and smaller values such as $\\alpha=0.1$ or $\\alpha=0.01$ for less aggressive smoothing.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Bayesian interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Add-$\\alpha$ smoothing can be interpreted through a symmetric Dirichlet prior on each class token vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_c\\sim \\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\alpha,\\ldots,\\alpha).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The smoothed estimate\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{N_{cj}+\\alpha}{N_c+\\alpha d}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is the posterior predictive probability of the next token being $v_j$ after observing the class-specific token counts.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Smoothing as regularization.**  Add-$\\alpha$ smoothing shrinks the estimated token distribution away from the boundary of the simplex.  No token receives probability zero, so every class remains possible for every document.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Log-scores and numerical stability\n",
        "\n",
        "The product\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi_c\\prod_{j=1}^d \\theta_{cj}^{x_j}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "can be extremely small.  In computation, multiplying many small probabilities may underflow to zero.  Taking logarithms avoids this problem and converts products into sums:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_c(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\log \\pi_c+\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j\\log \\theta_{cj}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The classifier is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat y\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\max_{c\\in\\mathcal{Y}} s_c(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the logarithm is increasing, maximizing the log-score is equivalent to maximizing the original posterior numerator.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Naive Bayes as a linear classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "The score for class $c$ can be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_c(x)=b_c+w_c^\\top x,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b_c=\\log \\pi_c,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "w_c=(\\log \\theta_{c1},\\ldots,\\log \\theta_{cd})^\\top.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus multinomial Naive Bayes is a linear classifier in count-vector space.\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary classification with classes $1$ and $0$, compare log-scores:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_1(x)-s_0(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\log\\frac{\\pi_1}{\\pi_0}\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j\\log\\frac{\\theta_{1j}}{\\theta_{0j}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The decision rule is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat y=1\n",
        "\\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad\n",
        "\\log\\frac{\\pi_1}{\\pi_0}\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j\\log\\frac{\\theta_{1j}}{\\theta_{0j}}>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The decision boundary is the hyperplane\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\frac{\\pi_1}{\\pi_0}\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j\\log\\frac{\\theta_{1j}}{\\theta_{0j}}=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "The weights\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\frac{\\theta_{1j}}{\\theta_{0j}}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "are interpretable.  A large positive value means token $v_j$ strongly favors class $1$ over class $0$.  A large negative value means the token strongly favors class $0$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relation to logistic regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Both Naive Bayes and logistic regression can produce linear decision boundaries, but they estimate different objects.\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes is a **generative** model.  It estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=c),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "P(X=x\\mid Y=c),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and then uses Bayes' theorem to obtain a classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression is a **discriminative** model.  It estimates the conditional probability directly:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=c\\mid X=x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary classification, logistic regression models\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid X=x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sigma(\\beta_0+\\beta^\\top x),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes may work well with small data because it imposes strong probabilistic structure.  Logistic regression may work better with enough labeled data because it directly optimizes conditional prediction.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bernoulli Naive Bayes\n",
        "\n",
        "The multinomial model uses token counts.  Another common model uses binary features:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_j=\\mathbf{1}\\{\\text{token }v_j\\text{ appears in the document}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For each class $c$, Bernoulli Naive Bayes estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\rho_{cj}=P(Z_j=1\\mid Y=c).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The class-conditional probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Z=z\\mid Y=c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{j=1}^d \\rho_{cj}^{z_j}(1-\\rho_{cj})^{1-z_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-score becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_c(z)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\log \\pi_c+\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d\n",
        "\\left[z_j\\log \\rho_{cj}+(1-z_j)\\log(1-\\rho_{cj})\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Bernoulli Naive Bayes models word presence or absence; multinomial Naive Bayes models word counts.  For longer documents, multinomial Naive Bayes is often more natural.  For short texts, binary presence features can be useful.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Worked example by hand\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the vocabulary is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{\\texttt{win},\\texttt{meeting},\\texttt{free}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "There are two classes:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{Y}=\\{\\text{spam},\\text{ham}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume the estimated parameters are:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\pi_{\\text{spam}}=0.4,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\widehat \\pi_{\\text{ham}}=0.6,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "| token | $\\widehat\\theta_{\\text{spam},j}$ | $\\widehat\\theta_{\\text{ham},j}$ |\n",
        "|---|---:|---:|\n",
        "| `win` | 0.50 | 0.05 |\n",
        "| `meeting` | 0.05 | 0.70 |\n",
        "| `free` | 0.45 | 0.25 |\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the document\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "free win free\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Its count vector is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(1,0,2).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The spam log-score is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log(0.4)+1\\log(0.50)+0\\log(0.05)+2\\log(0.45).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The ham log-score is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log(0.6)+1\\log(0.05)+0\\log(0.70)+2\\log(0.25).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since `win` and `free` are much more likely under the spam token distribution, the spam score will be larger.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: training from scratch\n",
        "\n",
        "The following code implements multinomial Naive Bayes without using a machine learning library.  The point is to see the mathematical quantities directly."
      ],
