        "\n",
        "## Statistical interpretation of smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "Smoothing changes a classifier in two ways.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, it prevents impossible events.  Every token receives positive probability in every class:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat \\theta_{cj}^{(\\alpha)}>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, it reduces the magnitude of extreme log-odds weights.  If a token appears many times in one class and zero times in another, the unsmoothed log-ratio would be infinite.  Smoothing makes the ratio finite:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\frac{N_{1j}+\\alpha}{N_{0j}+\\alpha}\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\log\\frac{N_0+\\alpha d}{N_1+\\alpha d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus smoothing acts like a regularizer on the classifier's feature weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Model assumptions and failure modes\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes works best when class-indicative tokens are strong and word order is not essential.  It can fail when meaning depends on syntax, negation, or context.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Failure mode 1: negation\n",
        "\n",
        "The phrases\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "good\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "not good\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "have different meanings.  A unigram Naive Bayes model may treat `good` as positive even when preceded by `not`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Possible remedy: add bigram features such as `not_good`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Failure mode 2: repeated correlated words\n",
        "\n",
        "Because Naive Bayes multiplies token probabilities, repeated correlated words may make the model overconfident.  The model behaves as though each occurrence contributes independent evidence.\n",
        "\n",
        "Possible remedy: use binary features, stronger smoothing, feature selection, or a discriminative model.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Failure mode 3: dataset leakage\n",
        "\n",
        "If the training data contains label artifacts, the classifier may learn shortcuts.  For example, if all spam examples contain a footer phrase not present at deployment time, the model may appear accurate in validation but fail in practice.\n",
